1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,087
LeetCode problem number one, and still

2
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:05,574
the most common warm-up question in interviews.

3
00:00:05,524 --> 00:00:08,889
Let's solve it the way an interviewer wants to see it.

4
00:00:19,148 --> 00:00:22,146
Given an array of numbers and a target, return the

5
00:00:22,146 --> 00:00:25,144
indices of the two numbers that add up to the target.

6
00:00:25,094 --> 00:00:29,625
There is exactly one answer, and you cannot use the same element twice.

7
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:34,397
For example, two and seven add up to nine, so we return zero and one.

8
00:00:38,156 --> 00:00:41,363
The brute force idea is to try every pair.

9
00:00:41,313 --> 00:00:44,012
For each i, check every j after it, and

10
00:00:44,012 --> 00:00:46,710
test whether the two values add up to the target.

11
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:49,691
It works, but it is quadratic time.

12
00:00:49,691 --> 00:00:53,489
With a hundred thousand numbers, that is billions of checks.

13
00:00:57,236 --> 00:00:59,422
Here is the key insight.

14
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:04,396
For every number x, the partner we need is target minus x, its complement.

15
00:01:04,396 --> 00:01:07,767
If we remember every value we have already seen, along with

16
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:11,138
its index, we can check for the complement in constant time.

17
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,664
So the plan is: keep a hash map from value to index.

18
00:01:16,614 --> 00:01:20,953
For each number, first check if its complement is already in the map.

19
00:01:20,953 --> 00:01:23,213
If it is, we are done.

20
00:01:23,213 --> 00:01:26,206
Otherwise, store the number and move on.

21
00:01:26,206 --> 00:01:30,725
Checking before inserting guarantees we never pair an element with itself.

22
00:01:34,492 --> 00:01:38,565
Let's trace three, two, four, with a target of six.

23
00:01:38,515 --> 00:01:43,034
At index zero we need three, the map is empty, so we store three.

24
00:01:43,034 --> 00:01:46,700
At index one we need four, not there, store two.

25
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:50,967
At index two we need two, and it is in the map at index one.

26
00:01:50,967 --> 00:01:53,250
We return one and two.

27
00:01:54,804 --> 00:01:57,266
And here is the Java solution.

28
00:01:57,216 --> 00:02:00,774
One loop, one hash map, and an early return.

29
00:02:00,774 --> 00:02:03,815
I have also kept the brute force version underneath, so

30
00:02:03,815 --> 00:02:06,855
you can compare them side by side in your interview prep.

31
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:14,693
Complexity: one pass over the array, so linear time.

32
00:02:14,643 --> 00:02:17,419
And linear extra space for the map.

33
00:02:17,419 --> 00:02:21,782
We trade memory for speed, which is almost always the right call here.

34
00:02:25,548 --> 00:02:27,902
Two pitfalls before you go.

35
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:31,031
Return indices, not values, and do not sort

36
00:02:31,031 --> 00:02:34,210
the array, because sorting destroys the original indices.

37
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:37,239
The full solution and tests are on GitHub.

38
00:02:37,239 --> 00:02:39,102
I am Waqar.

39
00:02:39,102 --> 00:02:42,804
Like and subscribe, and I will see you in the next problem.

